{"url": "https://www.aeaweb.org/conference/2023/program/paper/Kfredby2", "title": "Theory and Evidence", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "## Seasoned Equity Offerings, Limited Investor Attention, and Post-Announcement Drift: Theory and Evidence\n...\nWe develop a model of seasoned equity offerings (SEOs) under limited investor attention. While existing models of equity is sues assume that all investors pay immediate attention to SEO announcements, we assume that only a fraction of investors pay immediate attention, with the remaining fraction paying delayed attention. We develop three testable predictions from our the oretical model not generated by existing equity issue models. First, in addition to an announcement effect, there will a post announcement stock return drift following SEOs. Second, the announcement effect of an SEO will be increasing and the post announcement drift will be decreasing in the fraction of equity market investors paying immediate attention to the SEO an nouncement. Third, both the announcement effect and the post SEO drift will have predictive power for the post-SEO operating performance of firms. We test the above three predictions of our theoretical model using the media coverage of firms prior to SEO announcements as our proxy for investor attention and find con sistent evidence. Our baseline empirical results are robust to mak ing use of abnormal investor attention (instead of the actual in vestor attention) received by firms, allowing us to rule out the possibility that our results are driven by the characteristics of cer tain firms that receive greater investor attention compared", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 61438, "excerpt": "%PDF-1.5 %���� 3 0 obj > endobj 4 0 obj > /W [ 1 3 1 ] /Index [ 3 58 ] /Info 1 0 R /Root 5 0 R /Size 61 /Prev 129577 /ID [ ] >> stream x�cbd`�g`b``8 \"�/��F �q1��\u0000\"M�@��6��3\u0010i� \"ݻ�$�E �-� D*�I�$\u0010�p\u0003,� \"e���?\u0002�\u000f���`�\u0011L�\"H��0������]��8�H\u0000��\u0013� endstream endobj 5 0 obj > endobj 6 0 obj > stream x�c``\u0010g``������e#\u0003 �ج@�\u0000�P�������p>�q�X�Cæ��e� �dw�:�\u0010 > endobj 8 0 obj > stream x���ہ�8 ��k �zN5��\u00163�\u0004\u0002�,�2", "jev": {"concrete": 0.19, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.88, "market": "other", "us_long_only": 0.3, "recent": 0.21, "code": 0.04}, "priority": 0.011}
{"url": "https://doi.org/10.1080/10835547.2022.2118100", "title": "Do Designated Sales Agents in ATM Offerings Exploit Post-Earnings-Announcement Drift? Evidence from Real Estate Investment Trusts", "engine": "exa", "published": "2022-07-03", "snippet": "# Do Designated Sales Agents in ATM Offerings Exploit Post-Earnings-Announcement Drift? Evidence from Real Estate Investment Trusts\n...\nThis paper investigates whether designated sales agents hired to work on the At-the-Market (ATM) offerings act as liquidity traders and arbitrage apparent market inefficiencies such as post-earnings-announcement drift (PEAD). Those sales agents can compete to provide liquidity in the presence of asymmetric information and continuously sell their new equity shares at any time. We find that the supply of liquidity shocks from ATM offerings negatively impacts the cross-section of risk premia. Consistent with the prediction of a dynamic rational expectations model, the well-documented PEAD anomaly disappears in Real Estate Investment Trusts (REITs). When analyzing reversal magnitudes around earnings announcements, we show attenuated return reversals in ATM REITs compared with non-ATM REITs. Furthermore, institutional ownership, institutional herding, and adverse selection cannot explain future returns in firms with good earnings surprises below their expected levels.\n...\n- David Hartzell, Shawn D. Howton, Shelly Howton, Benjamin Scheick. Financial Flexibility and At‐the‐Market (ATM) Equity Offerings: Evidence from Real Estate Investment Trusts. Real Estate Economics. 2016;47(2):595-636. doi:10.1111/1540-6229.12131.\n...\n- Shawn D. Howton, Shelly Howton, Benjamin Scheick. Financial Flexibility and Investment: Evidence from REIT At‐the‐Market (ATM) ", "extra": {}, "read_via": "exa_fetch", "text_chars": 8389, "excerpt": "# Do Designated Sales Agents in ATM Offerings Exploit Post-Earnings-Announcement Drift? Evidence from Real Estate Investment Trusts URL: https://doi.org/10.1080/10835547.2022.2118100 Published: 2022-07-03 Author: Yu‐Jou Pai, Dazhi Zheng, Suyan Zheng # Do Designated Sales Agents in ATM Offerings Exploit Post-Earnings-Announcement Drift? Evidence from Real Estate Investment Trusts Journal of Real Es", "jev": {"concrete": 0.1, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.32, "market": "us_stocks_etfs", "us_long_only": 0.38, "recent": 0.03, "code": 0.03}, "priority": 0.01}
{"url": "https://eprints.gla.ac.uk/343877/2/343877.pdf", "title": "Cash-rich seasoned equity issuers", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "ABSTRACT We document that a substantial fraction of seasoned equity issuers have large pre offering excess cash holdings. Cash-rich seasoned equity issuers are not easily recon cilable with the traditional pecking order theory on security choice. We examine the role of market timing, agency costs, precautionary motives, repatriation taxes, catering incentives, real options, and negative cash flows in driving seasoned equity offerings (SEOs) by cash-rich firms. We find that cash-rich equity issuers are more overvalued, use the SEO proceeds more opportunistically, and have a worse post-SEO operating and long-term stock price performance than non-cash-rich issuers. However, we do not detect differences in SEO announcement returns between cash-rich and non-cash-rich issuers, suggesting investors underweight firms’ excess cash holdings when assessing an SEO announcement. Overall, our findings predominantly support a market timing motive for SEOs by cash-rich issuers, while other explanations receive mixed to no support. Our results are robust to alternative empirical specifications.\n...\nSeasoned equity offerings (SEOs) are an important corporate financing mechanism. In 2023 and the first half of 2024 alone, U.S. companies raised $188.9 billion in total via SEOs.1 A recent strand of the corporate finance literature attributes SEO activity to firms’ urgent cash needs. DeAngelo, DeAngelo, and Stulz (2010) find that in the year after the offering, 81.1% of U.S. seasoned equity issuers", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 381033, "excerpt": "%PDF-1.4 %���� 1 0 obj << /Type /Catalog /Version /1.5 /Pages 2 0 R /PageLabels 3 0 R /Metadata 4 0 R /Names 5 0 R /Outlines 6 0 R >> endobj 7 0 obj << /Author () /CreationDate (D:20250502235802+05'30') /Creator (LaTeX with hyperref) /Keywords () /ModDate (D:20250502235840+05'30') /Producer <4163726F6261742044697374696C6C657220382E312E30202857696E646F7773293B206D6F646966696564207573696E67206954657", "jev": {"concrete": 0.16, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.93, "market": "other", "us_long_only": 0.28, "recent": 0.38, "code": 0.04}, "priority": 0.01}
{"url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-financial-and-quantitative-analysis/article/abs/price-pressure-and-overnight-seasoned-equity-offerings/DE87F9B6B394706A5371AABDB87B2227", "title": "Price Pressure and Overnight Seasoned Equity Offerings", "engine": "exa", "published": "2018-04-05", "snippet": "Between 2009 and 2014, 75% of seasoned equity offerings (SEOs) were announced and issued overnight, compared to 27% between 2000 and 2008. Overnight issuers obtain a higher SEO offer price because they experience more favorable pre-offer returns. Consistent with these favorable returns being due to the avoidance of pre-issue selling pressure, non-overnight issuers experience a 2.5% pre-issue stock-price decline that reverses within 7 days. This post-issue reversal is increasing in SEO offer size and bigger following large pre-issue price declines. In contrast, returns following overnight offerings are less positive and unrelated to SEO offer size or pre-issue returns.\n\n---", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 27562, "excerpt": "Price Pressure and Overnight Seasoned Equity Offerings | Journal of Financial and Quantitative Analysis | Cambridge Core Skip to main content Accessibility help Login Alert Cancel Log in × &#215; Temporary Disruption We have implemented additional assurance measures to protect our systems. As a result, we have suspended some of our systems and services. We apologise for any temporary disruption so", "jev": {"concrete": 0.09, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.98, "market": "us_stocks_etfs", "us_long_only": 0.39, "recent": 0.02, "code": 0.03}, "priority": 0.009}
{"url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-financial-and-quantitative-analysis/article/etf-ownership-and-seasoned-equity-offerings/6648AC234604E1F25E030C97C2481EBE", "title": "ETF Ownership and Seasoned Equity Offerings | Journal of Financial and Quantitative Analysis | Cambridge Core", "engine": "exa", "published": "2023-04-11", "snippet": "This article investigates the impact of exchange-traded fund (ETF) ownership on seasoned equity offerings (SEOs). We find that increases to firms’ ETF ownership is positively related to their propensity to conduct an SEO. ETF ownership is also associated with less negative SEO announcement returns, smaller discounts, and better long-run stock returns. Our evidence is consistent with equity issuance following investor demand for stocks driven by greater participation in ETFs, suggesting a possible alternative source of market timing opportunity.\n...\nJournal of Financial and Quantitative Analysis, Volume 59, Issue 4, June 2024 , pp. 1821 - 1848\n...\nGustafson, M. T. “Price Pressure and Overnight Seasoned Equity Offerings.” Journal of Financial and Quantitative Analysis, 53 (2018), 837–866.10.1017/S0022109017001181 CrossRef Google Scholar\n...\n–185\n...\nMola, S., and Loughran, T.. “Discounting and Clustering in Seasoned Equity Offering Prices.” Journal of Financial and Quantitative Analysis, 39 (2004), 1–23.10.1017/S0022109000003860 CrossRef Google Scholar\n\n---", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 26841, "excerpt": "ETF Ownership and Seasoned Equity Offerings | Journal of Financial and Quantitative Analysis | Cambridge Core Skip to main content Accessibility help Login Alert Cancel Log in × &#215; Temporary Disruption We have implemented additional assurance measures to protect our systems. As a result, we have suspended some of our systems and services. We apologise for any temporary disruption some customer", "jev": {"concrete": 0.05, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.99, "market": "us_stocks_etfs", "us_long_only": 0.43, "recent": 0.28, "code": 0.03}, "priority": 0.006}
{"url": "https://mdpi-res.com/d_attachment/ijfs/ijfs-09-00036/article_deploy/ijfs-09-00036.pdf?version=1626147389", "title": "Seasoned Equity Offerings and Differences in Share-Price Impact by Firm Categories", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "Abstract: The seasoned equity offering (SEO) market plays a significant role in the economic develop\u0002ment of a country by providing liquidity for ongoing commercialization and innovation. This study\n...\nis a comprehensive analysis of 149 SEOs and their effect on share prices in Thailand between 2009\n...\nand 2019. SEOs are categorized based on their time categories (early, mid, and grown) and volume\n...\ncategories (small, medium, big, and super). Using the event study methodology (multi-factor model),\n...\nwe find that most SEOs under both categories have a negative cumulative abnormal return (CAR) in\n...\nthe window period. Ranking the types of SEOs reveals that grown SEOs have the highest proportion\n...\nof negative CAR under the time categories. Under the volume categories, medium SEOs show the\n...\nlargest share. The results were validated by regression assumption tests provided by Gnu Regres\u0002sion, Econometrics and Time-series Library, and correspond to established theories. The paper also\n...\ncontains an extensive literature review of studies examining the link between SEOs and share-price\n...\nunderpricing after their issuance. There is a relatively\n...\nExtensive literature on the general link between an SEO and the stock prices of companies\n...\naround its announcement and issuance found SEOs to be an unwelcome sign among\n...\ninvestors. Adverse post-announcement and post-issuance outcomes of SEOs have been\n...\nidentified as the central theme by several scholars. Asquith and M", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 154528, "excerpt": "%PDF-1.7 %���� 6 0 obj >stream xڭZY��\u0011~�_��pR#\u000eA���%�٣ֱcgg\\���\u0003Da$x)R�1��ק\u001b��5� ��֬@ �F���\u001b V�U���*�o�~\u0