{"url": "https://ideas.repec.org/a/kap/jrefec/v66y2023i2d10.1007_s11146-021-09821-9.html", "title": "The Cost of Financial Flexibility: Information Opacity, Agency Conflicts and REIT at-the-Market (ATM) Equity Offerings", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "The Cost of Financial Flexibility: Information Opacity, Agency Conflicts and REIT at-the-Market (ATM) Equity Offerings\n...\nUsing a sample of publicly traded U.S. REITs, we examine the impact of at-the-market (ATM) equity offerings on participating firms’ cost of capital. While ATM programs can improve a firm’s financial flexibility, capital market access, and market timing capabilities, they also may engender agency conflicts and increase the information opacity of the firm’s operations. Consistent with these adverse effects, we document a positive relation between ATM program availability and the implied cost of equity capital. These core findings are most pronounced for firms that are relatively opaque, thus providing further evidence of an information-based channel for the cost of capital effect.\n...\nFinancial flexibility; At-the-market (ATM) equity offerings; Cost of capital; Agency problems; Informational opacity; All these keywords\n\n---", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 16694, "excerpt": "The Cost of Financial Flexibility: Information Opacity, Agency Conflicts and REIT at-the-Market (ATM) Equity Offerings Download full text from publisher As the access to this document is restricted, you may want to for a different version of it. References listed on IDEAS - Ang, James & Smedema, Adam, 2011. \"Financial flexibility: Do firms prepare for recession?,\" Journal of Corporate Finance, Els", "jev": {"concrete": 0.03, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.99, "market": "us_stocks_etfs", "us_long_only": 0.39, "recent": 0.24, "code": 0.04}, "priority": 0.003}
{"url": "https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=2178052", "title": "At-the-Market (ATM) Offerings", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "We study “At-The-Market” (ATM) equity offerings, which are direct share issuances sold in the secondary market that forgo underwriters and “dribble-out” shares over time rather than raising them all at once. Enabled in 2008, their use has increased dramatically, and in 2016 their incidence and total proceeds were respectively 63% and 26% of that for SEOs. Determinants of firms’ choice between ATMs and SEOs are consistent with the costly certification hypothesis of Chemmanur and Fulghieri (1994). We also find that 65% of ATM proceeds are used to stockpile cash compared to 84% of SEO proceeds.\n...\n**Keywords:**At-the-market offering; ATM; seasoned equity offering; equity issuance; certification\n...\n**JEL Classification:**G32, G34, G38\n\n---", "extra": {}, "read_via": "exa_fetch", "text_chars": 5362, "excerpt": "# At-the-Market (ATM) Offerings URL: https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=2178052 At-the-Market (ATM) Offerings by Matthew T. Billett, Ioannis V. Floros, Jon A. Garfinkel :: SSRN Skip to main content ** ** PDF iconDownload This Paper Open PDF in Browser **Add Paper to My Library Share: * ** * ** * ** * ** Permalink Using these links will ensure access to this page indefinitely Copy ", "jev": {"concrete": 0.03, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.97, "market": "us_stocks_etfs", "us_long_only": 0.32, "recent": 0.03, "code": 0.03}, "priority": 0.003}
{"url": "https://law-journals-books.vlex.com/vid/do-cost-behaviors-affect-1049457306", "title": "Do Cost Behaviors Affect Cost of Equity: Evidence from Seasoned Equity Offering? - vLex United States", "engine": "exa", "published": "2024-09-16", "snippet": "| Published date | 01 August 2023 |\n| --- | --- |\n| Author | He Xiao |\n| Date | 01 August 2\n...\nhttp://doi.org/10.1111/ajfs.12441 |\n...\nThis study investigates the impact of ﬁrms’ cost behavior on their seasoned equity offering\n...\n(SEO) performance. The empirical ﬁndings show that cost-stickier SEO ﬁrms experience\n...\nmore negative SEO cumulative abnormal returns (CARs). The economic mechanism of this\n...\nassociation is that cost-stickier SEO ﬁrms deal with higher information asymmetry and issue\n...\nfewer debts, which reduce their SEO CARs. SEO ﬁrm cost behavior also affects corporate\n...\nSEO decisions and announcement returns in different life cycles. Among the three economic\n...\nmechanisms, the information asymmetry channel has been found as the dominant one. This\n...\nstudy also indicates that cost-stickier ﬁrms are less likely to conduct SEOs than their counter-\n...\nparts. In contrast, the motivation for Chinese SEO issuance for cost-stickier ﬁrms is market\n...\ntiming rather than adjusting the capital structure or ﬁnancing for investment and growth.\n...\nAdditionally, the negative impact of cost stickiness on corporate SEO returns is less pro-\n...\nnounced for ﬁrms associated with industry-specialized auditors and greater corporate gover-\n...\nnance capacity but more substantial for SEO ﬁrms with higher free cash ﬂow. All main\n...\nof endogenous tests\n...\nSeasoned equity offerings (SEOs) are substantial external ﬁnancing channels in\n...\nthe modern ﬁnancial market (e.g. Bo et ", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 16821, "excerpt": "Do Cost Behaviors Affect Cost of Equity: Evidence from Seasoned Equity Offering? | Published date | 01 August 2023 | | Author | He Xiao | | Date | 01 August 2023 | | DOI | http://doi.org/10.1111/ajfs.12441 | Do Cost Behaviors Affect Cost of Equity: Evidence from Seasoned Equity Offering? He Xiao* Division of Business and Management, Beijing Normal University-Hong Kong Baptist University United Int", "jev": {"concrete": 0.04, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.7, "market": "china_a_shares", "us_long_only": 0.29, "recent": 0.31, "code": 0.03}, "priority": 0.002}
